course: dictionary
lesson: 1118
title: Bread: one word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Bread is a noun for a food made from flour, water and usually yeast, mixed into a dough and baked in an oven. You cannot count bread itself, so you say some bread, but you can count loaves and slices: «Before a loaf of bread reaches you, many people work on it.»
explain: Context helps you spot which meaning is being used. In «Leave a slice of bread in a warm, damp place and in a few days fuzzy spots of mould appear», the clues are slice, damp and mould, so bread means the food. Ask yourself what word would fit, then try your meaning in the sentence.
explain: Bread can also be an old-fashioned slang word for money. Phrases like bread and butter and the best thing since sliced bread show how often bread appears in everyday talk. If the sentence is about shopping or paying, check whether money fits better than food.
fact: Bread is a food made from flour, water and usually yeast.
fact: Bread comes in loaves, rolls and slices.
fact: Yeast makes gas bubbles that make bread rise: «They trap the gas, so the dough swells, and every hole in a slice of bread is where a bubble once was.»
fact: «Breads» means different kinds of bread.
fact: In French, «le pain» means bread.
exercise: A sandwich uses 2 slices of bread. How many slices do 3 sandwiches use? | 2 | *6 | 9 | 3
exercise: «Leave a slice of bread in a warm, damp place and in a few days fuzzy spots of mould appear.» What grows on the bread? | Yeast dough | Gas bubbles | Flour | *Mould
exercise: Which phrase is correct for one loaf? | Breads of one loaf | A bread of loaf | Some bread | *A loaf of bread
test: In «a slice of bread», what does slice mean? | A sharp cut in the crust | A kind of knife | A loud noise | *A thin piece cut from a loaf
test: «He has plenty of bread», said in an old-fashioned way, means he has | A big family | Lots of food | A cake shop | *Lots of money
exam: What does yeast do to dough? | Stops it from baking | *Makes gas bubbles so the bread rises | Freezes it | Turns it blue
